WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.120
Safe binary validation starts before individual fields are decoded.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:11.140
The parser needs a reliable view of how many bytes are actually available and how much capacity exists in the destination buffer.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:20.480
File size, message length, and transport metadata are useful inputs, but they should not be treated as proof that a future read will deliver exactly that amount of data.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.060
Many C APIs can return fewer bytes than requested.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:32.680
A partial read may be normal at end of file, may reflect an interrupted operation, or may indicate that data arrived in smaller pieces than expected.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:37.300
The important rule is simple: do not use bytes that have not actually been read.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:45.520
Check the return value, update the number of available bytes, and treat missing data as a validation failure rather than a surprising internal condition.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:49.020
Field readers should verify that enough bytes remain before reading.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:56.520
If a header requires four bytes for a magic value and two bytes for a version, the parser checks those limits before touching the memory.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:02.340
The same pattern applies to arrays, records, optional sections, and nested structures.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:09.120
A helper such as a cursor with a current position and remaining length can make these checks consistent and easier to audit.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:12.140
Buffer capacity and data length are different facts.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.080
Capacity is how much storage was allocated.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.420
Data length is how much valid input was received.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:26.560
Mixing them leads to code that reads uninitialized bytes, parses stale data, or believes a buffer is complete because it is large enough.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.740
Keep those values separate in names and in logic.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:31.880
End-of-input should be ordinary.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:39.460
A truncated file or short message is not an exceptional mystery; it is one of the most common invalid inputs a parser will see.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:47.120
Handling it cleanly means returning a clear validation failure, avoiding partial object construction, and leaving caller state predictable.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:52.300
Finally, binary data is not text unless the format says it is text and defines the rules.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:58.840
A byte sequence may contain zeros, invalid encodings, embedded separators, or no terminator at all.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:08.900
If a field is later treated as a C string, the parser must verify length, encoding expectations if relevant, and termination or create a safe terminated copy deliberately.
